          "content": "6.825: Final Exam"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.214,
            0.295,
            0.784,
            0.325
          ],
          "content": "There are 130 points total. Points for individual problems are indicated in bold."
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "bbox": [
            0.215,
            0.347,
            0.336,
            0.366
          ],
          "level": 2,
          "content": "1 Search"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.214,
            0.379,
            0.784,
            0.456
          ],
          "content": "(10) You’re a taxi driver. Your taxi can hold 4 passengers. Passengers pay a flat fee for a ride to the airport, so goal is to pick up 4 passengers and take them to the airport in the smallest number of miles. Your world can be modeled as a graph of locations with distances between them. Some, but not all, of the locations have passengers that you can pick up."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.214,
            0.467,
            0.589,
            0.482
          ],
          "content": "a. Describe the state space of this search problem."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.213,
            0.492,
            0.686,
            0.508
          ],
          "content": "b. What would be a good cost function for this search problem?"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.215,
            0.517,
            0.784,
            0.579
          ],
          "content": "c. Now, consider a case where passengers have to pay according to how far away they are from the airport when they’re picked up (note: they don’t pay according to how long a ride they take in your taxi, but according to the length of the shortest path from their pickup-point to the airport)."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.242,
            0.583,
            0.589,
            0.598
          ],
          "content": "Describe the state space of this search problem."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.213,
            0.607,
            0.782,
            0.639
          ],
          "content": "d. What would be a good cost function for this version of the problem? You still have a desire to save gas."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.214,
            0.648,
            0.783,
            0.679
          ],
          "content": "e. Is uniform cost search guaranteed to find the optimal solution in either or both versions of the problem? Why or why not?"
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "bbox": [
            0.215,
            0.701,
            0.436,
            0.72
          ],
          "level": 2,
          "content": "2 FOL Semantics"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.214,
            0.732,
            0.783,
            0.779
          ],
          "content": "(6) Consider a world with objects A, B, and C. We’ll look at a logical languge with constant symbols X, Y , and Z, function symbols f and g, and predicate symbols $p ,   q ,$ and r. Consider the following interpretation:"
        },
        {
          "type": "equation",
          "bbox": [
            0.238,
            0.787,
            0.493,
            0.803
          ],
          "content": "\\bullet I (X) = \\mathbf {A}, I (Y) = \\mathbf {A}, I (Z) = \\mathbf {B}",
          "image_source": "images/page_0_equation_12.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "equation",
          "bbox": [
            0.239,
            0.812,
            0.485,
            0.829
          ],
          "content": "\\bullet I (f) = \\{\\langle \\mathbf {A}, \\mathbf {B} \\rangle , \\langle \\mathbf {B}, \\mathbf {C} \\rangle , \\langle \\mathbf {C}, \\mathbf {C} \\rangle \\}",
          "image_source": "images/page_0_equation_13.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "equation",
          "bbox": [
            0.239,
            0.838,
            0.363,
            0.854
          ],
          "content": "\\bullet I (p) = \\{\\mathbf {A}, \\mathbf {B} \\}",
          "image_source": "images/page_0_equation_14.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "page_number",
          "bbox": [
            0.494,
            0.877,
            0.505,
            0.889
          ],
          "content": "1"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "page_idx": 1,
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.239,
            0.158,
            0.341,
            0.175
          ],
          "content": "$I ( q ) = \\{ \\mathbf { C } \\}$"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.239,
            0.183,
            0.483,
            0.2
          ],
          "content": "• I(r) = {hB, Ai, hC, Bi, hC, Ci}"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.214,
            0.209,
            0.782,
            0.239
          ],
          "content": "For each of the following sentences, say whether it is true or false in the given interpretation I:"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.215,
            0.249,
            0.304,
            0.265
          ],
          "content": "a. $q ( f ( Z ) )$"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.214,
            0.274,
            0.303,
            0.289
          ],
          "content": "b. $r ( X , Y )$"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.215,
            0.299,
            0.344,
            0.314
          ],
          "content": "c. $\\exists w . f ( w ) = Y$"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.214,
            0.325,
            0.349,
            0.34
          ],
          "content": "d. $\\forall w . r ( f ( w ) , w )$"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.215,
            0.35,
            0.512,
            0.365
          ],
          "content": "e. $\\forall u , v . r ( u , v ) \\to ( \\forall w . r ( u , w ) \\to v = w )$"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.217,
            0.374,
            0.512,
            0.39
          ],
          "content": "f. $\\forall u , v . r ( u , v ) \\to ( \\forall w . r ( w , v ) \\to u = w )$"
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "bbox": [
            0.214,
            0.413,
            0.434,
            0.432
          ],
          "level": 2,
          "content": "3 Interpretations"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.213,
            0.444,
            0.783,
            0.489
          ],
          "content": "(6) Using the same set of symbols as in the previous problem, for each group of sentences below, provide an interpretation that makes the sentences true, or show that it’s impossible."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.214,
            0.5,
            0.419,
            0.515
          ],
          "content": "a. $\\exists w . p ( w ) \\land \\exists w . q ( w )$"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.262,
            0.52,
            0.405,
            0.535
          ],
          "content": "$\\neg \\exists w . p ( w ) \\land q ( w )$"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.262,
            0.539,
            0.43,
            0.555
          ],
          "content": "$\\forall u . p ( u ) \\rightarrow \\exists v . r ( u , v )$"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.213,
            0.565,
            0.375,
            0.58
          ],
          "content": "b. $\\forall u . \\exists v . r ( u , v )$"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.263,
            0.585,
            0.379,
            0.6
          ],
          "content": "$\\exists u , v . \\neg r ( u , v )$"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.262,
            0.605,
            0.448,
            0.621
          ],
          "content": "$\\forall v . ( \\exists u . r ( u , v ) ) \\leftrightarrow p ( v ) )$"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.215,
            0.63,
            0.435,
            0.646
          ],
          "content": "c. $\\forall u , v . ( p ( v ) \\to r ( u , v ) )$"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.263,
            0.65,
            0.379,
            0.665
          ],
          "content": "$\\exists u , v . \\neg r ( u , v )$"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.263,
            0.671,
            0.336,
            0.686
          ],
          "content": "$\\exists v . p ( v )$"
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "bbox": [
            0.215,
            0.709,
            0.387,
            0.727
          ],
          "level": 2,
          "content": "4 Unification"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.214,
            0.74,
            0.782,
            0.77
          ],
          "content": "(6) For each pair of literals below, specify a most general unifier, or indicate that they are not unifiable."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.214,
            0.779,
            0.422,
            0.796
          ],
          "content": "a. $r ( f ( x ) , y )$ and $r ( z , g ( w ) )$"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.214,
            0.805,
            0.419,
            0.821
          ],
          "content": "b. $r(f(x),x)$ and $r ( y , g ( y ) )$"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.215,
            0.83,
            0.434,
            0.846
          ],
          "content": "c. $r(a,C,a)$ and $r(f(x),x,y)$"
        },
        {
          "type": "page_number",
          "bbox": [
            0.493,
            0.877,
            0.505,
            0.889
          ],
          "content": "2"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "page_idx": 2,
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "bbox": [
