            0.747
          ],
          "content": "(13) You’re an olympic skier. In practice today, you fell down and hurt your ankle. Based on the results of an x-ray, the doctor thinks that it’s broken with probability 0.2. So, the question is, should you ski in the race tomorrow?"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.214,
            0.748,
            0.784,
            0.822
          ],
          "content": "If you ski, you think you’ll win with probability 0.1. If your leg is broken and you ski on it, then you’ll damage it further. So, your utilities are as follows: if you win the race and your leg isn’t broken, +100; if you win and your leg is broken, +50; if you lose and your leg isn’t broken 0; if you lose and your leg is broken -50."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.214,
            0.824,
            0.782,
            0.853
          ],
          "content": "If you don’t ski, then if your leg is broken your utility is -10, and if it isn’t, it’s 0."
        },
        {
          "type": "page_number",
          "bbox": [
            0.494,
            0.877,
            0.505,
            0.889
          ],
          "content": "5"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "page_idx": 5,
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.214,
            0.159,
            0.526,
            0.175
          ],
          "content": "a. Draw the decision tree for this problem."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.213,
            0.183,
            0.779,
            0.199
          ],
          "content": "b. Evaluate the tree, indicating the best action choice and its expected utility."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.213,
            0.209,
            0.784,
            0.269
          ],
          "content": "You might be able to gather some more information about the state of your leg by having more tests. You might be able to gather more information about whether you’ll win the race by talking to your coach or the TV sports commentators."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.214,
            0.277,
            0.783,
            0.309
          ],
          "content": "c. Compute the expected value of perfect information about the state of your leg."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.213,
            0.317,
            0.783,
            0.348
          ],
          "content": "d. Compute the expected value of perfect information about whether you’ll win the race."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.214,
            0.356,
            0.784,
            0.402
          ],
          "content": "In the original statement of the problem, the probability that your leg is broken and the probability that you’ll win the race are independent. That’s a pretty unreasonable assumption."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.216,
            0.41,
            0.784,
            0.441
          ],
          "content": "f. Is it possible to use a decision tree in the case that the probability that you’ll win the race depends on whether your leg is broken?"
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "bbox": [
            0.215,
            0.462,
            0.586,
            0.481
          ],
          "level": 2,
          "content": "14 Markov Decision Processes"
        },
        {
          "type": "image",
          "bbox": [
            0.229,
            0.519,
            0.536,
            0.701
          ],
          "content": "",
          "captions": [],
          "footnotes": [],
          "image_source": "images/page_5_image_8.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.214,
            0.709,
            0.784,
            0.77
          ],
          "content": "(10) What are the values of the states in the following MDP, assuming $\\gamma =$ 0.9? In order to keep the diagram for being too complicated, we’ve drawn the transition probabilities for action 1 in one figure and the transition probabilities for action 2 in another. The rewards for the states are the same in both cases."
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "bbox": [
            0.215,
            0.791,
            0.464,
            0.81
          ],
          "level": 2,
          "content": "15 Neural networks"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.214,
            0.823,
            0.784,
            0.855
          ],
          "content": "(9) I have a collection of data, $d _ { i } ,$ each element of which is a number between 0 and 1. I want to fit a parameter p to the data by minimizing mean squared"
        },
        {
          "type": "page_number",
          "bbox": [
            0.494,
            0.877,
            0.505,
            0.889
          ],
          "content": "6"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "page_idx": 6,
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.213,
            0.159,
            0.782,
            0.19
          ],
          "continues_prev": true,
          "content": "error. $\\mathrm { S o } ,$ the error for parameter $p$ is the sum, over all the data, of the squared difference between p and the data point:"
        },
        {
          "type": "equation",
          "bbox": [
            0.413,
            0.198,
            0.582,
            0.23
          ],
          "content": "\\mathrm{err} (p) = \\sum_ {i} (p - d _ {i}) ^ {2}.",
          "image_source": "images/page_6_equation_1.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.213,
            0.24,
            0.783,
            0.27
          ],
          "content": "Which, if any, of the following are gradient descent rules for updating p based on data?"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.214,
            0.28,
            0.37,
            0.296
          ],
          "content": "a. $p \\gets p + . 1 ( p - d _ { i } )$"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.214,
            0.304,
            0.371,
            0.32
          ],
          "content": "b. $p \\gets p - . 1 ( p - d _ { i } )$"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.215,
            0.329,
            0.396,
            0.346
          ],
          "content": "c. $\\textstyle p \\leftarrow p + . 1 \\sum _ { i } ( p - d _ { i } )$"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.214,
            0.353,
            0.396,
            0.37
          ],
          "content": "d. $\\textstyle p \\leftarrow p - . 1 \\sum _ { i } ( p - d _ { i } )$"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.215,
            0.378,
            0.359,
            0.396
          ],
          "content": "e. $\\textstyle p \\leftarrow p + . 1 \\sum _ { i } d _ { i }$"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.217,
            0.403,
            0.359,
            0.42
          ],
          "content": "f. $\\textstyle p \\leftarrow p - . 1 \\sum _ { i } d _ { i }$"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.214,
            0.427,
            0.378,
            0.445
          ],
          "content": "g. $p \\gets p + . 1 ( p - d _ { i } ) ^ { 2 }$"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.214,
            0.451,
            0.378,
            0.469
          ],
          "content": "h. $p \\gets p - . 1 ( p - d _ { i } ) ^ { 2 }$"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.217,
            0.476,
            0.361,
            0.495
          ],
          "content": "i. $\\textstyle p \\leftarrow \\sum _ { i } ( p - d _ { i } ) ^ { 2 }$"
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "bbox": [
            0.215,
            0.516,
            0.499,
            0.535
          ],
          "level": 2,
          "content": "16 Misc. True or False"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.215,
            0.546,
            0.252,
            0.562
          ],
          "content": "(14)"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.214,
            0.574,
            0.593,
            0.59
          ],
          "content": "a. Walksat sometimes moves to a worse assignment."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.212,
            0.598,
            0.604,
            0.614
          ],
          "content": "b. $\\mathrm { A } ^ { * }$ finds better solutions than uniform cost search."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.214,
            0.624,
            0.782,
            0.654
          ],
          "content": "c. If sentence $\\phi$ entails sentence $\\psi ,$ then $\\phi$ is true in only a (possibly proper) subset of the interpretations in which $\\psi$ is true."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.213,
            0.663,
            0.784,
            0.694
          ],
          "content": "d. If the premises are consistent, first-order resolution terminates without a contradiction."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.215,
            0.704,
            0.783,
            0.733
          ],
          "content": "e. If first-order resolution terminates without a contradiction, the premises are consistent."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.216,
            0.743,
            0.783,
            0.774
          ],
          "content": "f. The GraphPlan graph can have size exponential in the size of the domain description."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.214,
            0.783,
            0.783,
            0.814
          ],
          "content": "g. The GraphPlan algorithm runs in time exponential in the size of the domain description."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.213,
            0.823,
            0.782,
            0.854
          ],
          "content": "h. If A and B are independent, then A and B are conditionally independent given C."
        },
        {
          "type": "page_number",
          "bbox": [
            0.494,
            0.877,
            0.505,
            0.889
          ],
          "content": "7"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "page_idx": 7,
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.218,
            0.158,
            0.783,
            0.189
          ],
          "content": "i. The variable elimination algorithm runs in time polynomial in the size of the CPTs in the network."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.216,
            0.199,
            0.702,
            0.215
          ],
          "content": "j. The EM algorithm assigns hidden variables their optimal values."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.214,
            0.224,
            0.783,
            0.255
          ],
          "content": "k. Using the Bayesian correction in parameter estimation means that we no longer get the model that maximizes the likelihood of the data."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.218,
            0.265,
            0.779,
            0.28
          ],
          "content": "l. It is rational to buy insurance if you’re risk-seeking in the domain of losses."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.209,
            0.29,
            0.782,
            0.32
          ],
          "content": "m. The discounted model of optimality in MDPs is inappropriate for agents that have a known finite lifetime."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.214,
            0.33,
            0.782,
            0.359
          ],
          "content": "n. In reinforcement learning, the agent needs to know the reward function in advance."
        },
        {
          "type": "page_number",
          "bbox": [
            0.494,
            0.877,
            0.505,
            0.889
          ],
          "content": "8"
        }
      ]
    }
